PN,49606
SC,0006
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F496A1775044735
OL,1,Vk4                               G6/27
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGROGERS HERALDS BATTLE FOR
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGSTATE EDUCATION
OL,5, Teachers are facing a battle for the
OL,6, state education system against
OL,7, "privatisation by stealth", the new
OL,8, president of the NAS/UWT has said.
OL,10,FAt the teaching union's annual
OL,11,Fconference in Bournemouth, Susan Rogers
OL,12,Fsaid countless injustices were being
OL,13,Fperpetrated on the system.
OL,14,F
OL,15,FShe warned that differences in funding
OL,16,Fwere widening educational opportunities
OL,17,Fand said the country was moving back
OL,18,Ftowards Disraeli's two nations.
OL,20,F"The education system is no longer safe
OL,21,Fin this government's hands," she said.
OL,22,F
OL,23,D]FFromG01-04-91F/ teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,497,190,120,250,0,100
PN,49607
SC,0007
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F496A1775044735
OL,1,Vk4                               G7/27
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGTEACHERS "DEMORALISED"
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGWARNS UNION
OL,4,D
OL,5, The Government should "make up its
OL,6, mind" on the future of education, a
OL,7, teachers' leader has warned.
OL,8,F
OL,9,FPeter Smith, general secretary of the
OL,10,FAssistant Masters and Mistresses
OL,11,FAssociation, said teachers were
OL,12,Fextremely demoralised.
OL,13,F
OL,14,FHe accused the Government of appearing
OL,15,Fto move too fast in too many directions
OL,16,Fat once, and said AMMA's members were
OL,17,Fsaying: "stop mucking us about".
OL,18,F
OL,19,FAbout 600 delegates from the 130,000-
OL,20,Fmember union are to meet in Eastbourne
OL,21,Ftomorrow for their annual conference.
OL,23,D]FFromG01-04-91F/ teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,497,190,120,250,0,100
PN,49608
SC,0008
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F496A1775044735
OL,1,Vk4                               G8/27
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGBRITAIN MAY HIT BOTTOM
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGOF RECESSION SOON
OL,4,D
OL,5, The bottom of the recession may be in
OL,6, sight, says the director-general of the
OL,7, Confederation of British Industry.
OL,9,FSpeaking on BBC radio, John Banham said
OL,10,Fthe economy could turn around in the
OL,11,Fsecond half of the year if levels of
OL,12,Finflation continued to improve.
OL,13,F
OL,14,FBut he warned times were still very
OL,15,Fdifficult for many businesses and the
OL,16,Fsituation was worsening.
OL,17,F
OL,18,FMeanwhile, Shadow Chancellor John Smith
OL,19,Fsaid the outlook for investment was
OL,20,Fvery serious and warned the recession
OL,21,Fhad damaged the economy at many levels.
OL,22,F
OL,23,D]FFromG01-04-91F/ teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,497,190,120,250,0,100
PN,49609
SC,0009
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F496A1775044735
OL,1,Vk4                               G9/27
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGLABOUR WARNS OF FURTHER
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGREDUNDANCIES
OL,4,D
OL,5, Labour's trade and industry spokesman
OL,6, has warned that Britain could face a
OL,7, "spring and summer of redundancies".
OL,9,FGordon Brown said continued rises in
OL,10,Funemployment might be prevented if the
OL,11,FGovernment took immediate action to
OL,12,Fboost manufacturing investment.
OL,14,FHe said Labour's own business survey,
OL,15,Fto be issued soon, showed that more
OL,16,Fthan 5,000 businesses had gone under in
OL,17,Fthe first quarter of 1991.
OL,18,F
OL,19,FHe estimated over 150,000 jobs had been
OL,20,Flost this year, ensuring the economy
OL,21,Fwould be the biggest pre-election issue
OL,22,F
OL,23,D]FFromG01-04-91F/ teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
FL,497,190,120,250,0,100
PN,49610
SC,0010
CT,25,T
PS,c008
OL,0,        ECIMSBWebfax 2   F496A1775044735
OL,1,Vk4                              G10/27
OL,2,Vj5{5jj5~sGALBANIAN COMMUNISTS
OL,3,Vj5op0ozz5pGCLAIM VICTORY
OL,4,D
OL,5, In Albania, the communists have claimed
OL,6, victory in the first free elections
OL,7, since the Second World War.
OL,8,F
OL,9,FThe communists - now called the Party
OL,10,Fof Labour - say they will win
OL,11,Ftwo-thirds of the parliamentary seats.
OL,13,FThey soundly beat the opposition in the
OL,14,Fcountryside, but lost heavily in the
OL,15,Fcities - President Ramiz Alia lost his
OL,16,Fseat in Tirana, the capital.
OL,18,FThe opposition Democrats are likely to
OL,19,Fwin no more than 75 of the 250 seats,
OL,20,Fbelow the 33% needed to force reforms.
OL,21,F
OL,22,D]F This Ceefax page is fromG01-04-91
OL,23,D]F Much more at: teletextarchive.com
OL,24,ANext PageBT'text FilesCRewindFReg Radio
